  "trends": "AI market is not emerging - it has emerged. The data from 2024 and 2025 is unambiguous: the city has moved from a secondary IT hub to a primary destination for AI investment, talent, and infrastructure. Here is what that means for businesses making decisions about AI right now.Hyderabad Is Becoming India's Second-Largest Data Centre HubHyderabad's operational data centre capacity grew from 60.9 MW in 2022 to 151.4 MW by the end of 2025 - among the fastest growth rates of any major Indian city, according to Knight Frank's Data Centre Market Update 2025. The city has a committed and early-stage development pipeline of approximately 1.9 GW. In January 2025, Telangana signed a $1.15 billion MOU with CtrlS Datacenters to establish an AI data centre cluster expected to generate 3,000 jobs. For businesses running AI workloads, this means low-latency cloud compute, GPU access, and vector database infrastructure are available and expanding rapidly in Hyderabad's own backyard.The Telangana Government Has Made AI Infrastructure a Policy PriorityTelangana's AI investments are not rhetorical - they are structural. The government launched a 200-acre AI City in September 2024, featuring high-performance computing facilities, an AI-focused university, and co-located R&D labs. The Telangana Data Exchange (TGDeX) - India's first state-led digital public infrastructure for AI - launched in July 2025 with JICA, IISc Bengaluru, and Boston Consulting Group as founding partners. It offers curated datasets and subsidised GPU access to AI startups, reducing the compute cost barrier that typically limits early-stage AI development. In February 2025, Telangana partnered with Google to launch an AI Accelerator in Hyderabad focused on agriculture, mobility, education, and governance. T-Hub 2.0 - the world's largest startup incubation centre at 572,000 square feet - accommodates 1,000+ startups and has supported 2,000+ companies raising over $2 billion in funding.Hyderabad Now Has 450+ GCCs and That Number Is GrowingHyderabad hosts over 450 Global Capability Centres, and recent trends show it attracting more new GCC setups than most other Indian cities. Microsoft's Hyderabad campus - its largest data centre region in India - employs 7,000 people and leased an additional 265,000 square feet in 2025 specifically for AI and cloud operations. Google, Meta, JPMorgan Chase, Qualcomm, SAP, Novartis, DBS Bank, Goldman Sachs, and Deloitte all have substantial engineering operations here. The talent that rotates through these GCCs into the broader market creates a hiring pool with genuine production AI experience - which is precisely what makes Hyderabad competitive.$571 Million in Startup Funding in 2024 Signals Ecosystem MaturityTracxn data shows $571 million invested in Telangana tech startups in 2024 across 81 rounds. Late-stage deals accounted for $297 million - signalling that Hyderabad's startup ecosystem is not just producing early-stage companies, but maturing them to scale. HealthTech attracted nearly $300 million, FinTech around $105 million. AI startups raised $1.8 billion across 2019-2025. The Machine Learning and AI Technology Hub (MATH) at T-Hub concurrently incubates 100 AI startups. This is not speculative activity - it is a functioning AI startup economy.Hyderabad Produces 300,000+ Tech Graduates AnnuallyIIT Hyderabad, IIIT Hyderabad, Osmania University, BITS Pilani Hyderabad, the Indian School of Business, and a dense network of engineering colleges produce more than 300,000 technology graduates per year - second only to Bangalore in India. Salary expectations and attrition rates remain more manageable than Bangalore, which makes Hyderabad increasingly attractive for GCCs and startups building AI teams at scale. The AI talent pipeline here is real, and it is growing.21% of India's Total Tech Leasing Happened in Hyderabad in H1 2025By the first half of 2025, Hyderabad's tech-sector office leasing reached 2.3 million square feet - 21% of India's total. HITEC City, the Financial District, and Kokapet SEZ remain the primary drivers. This expansion in physical infrastructure reflects real business decisions by companies putting serious operations into Hyderabad. AI development teams, engineering labs, and GCC expansion floors do not lease space on speculation - they lease it because they are building.DPDP Act Compliance Is Now a Business Requirement, Not a Future ConcernIndia's Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 is enforceable. For AI systems handling user queries, patient data, employee records, or financial transactions - which covers most meaningful enterprise AI use cases - DPDP Act compliance is not optional. Hyderabad's pharma, healthtech, and fintech sectors are particularly exposed. AI systems built without proper data governance architecture face regulatory, legal, and reputational risks that are tangible and growing. Every AI system we build for Hyderabad clients is architectured for DPDP Act compliance from day one.  OUR AI DEVELOPMENT PROCESS",
